# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal financial data — NEVER commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw brokerage exports contain real account activity, positions and dollar
# amounts. They stay on disk and are read by the app, but are not tracked, so
# that pushing this repo anywhere can never leak them.

# `data/*.csv` matches the top level only. Broker exports arrive in subdirectories too -- the user
# dropped five into `data/rawData/` on 2026-08-14 and `git check-ignore` confirmed none of them were
# covered, so a `git add -A` would have committed real brokerage history. The `**` forms below are
# the rule that was meant all along; the flat ones are kept because removing them would rely on
# nobody's git version treating `**` differently.
data/**/*.csv
data/**/*.xlsx
data/**/*.xls
data/**/*.ofx
data/**/*.qfx
data/**/*.pdf
data/*.csv
data/*.xlsx
data/*.xls
data/*.ofx
data/*.qfx
data/*.pdf
data/statements/
data/1099/
# The account manifest names the user's real accounts and their tax treatments. It is not an
# export, but it describes them, and "rh-roth / roth_ira" is exactly the kind of detail the rule
# above exists to keep out of a repository.
data/accounts.toml

# Redacted fixtures built for tests ARE tracked (see tests/fixtures/).
!tests/fixtures/**

# `data/rounds/` IS TRACKED, DELIBERATELY, AND MUST STAY THAT WAY.
#
# It holds the strategy programme's round records — the brief each round was run under, the cards
# submitted against it, every run's trade log, and the reports written from them. It lives under
# `data/` because the application *writes* it (`app.tournament.jobs` creates a round there, and
# `Settings.rounds_dir` / `INVDASH_ROUNDS_DIR` is how the Docker image reaches it through the `/data`
# mount), not because it is derived or personal. None of it is financial data: no positions, no
# account numbers, no dollar balances of the user's own — it is backtest output over public prices.
#
# The hazard this comment exists for: `data/` carries a NEVER-COMMIT banner at the top of this file,
# so the natural instinct on seeing a new subdirectory here is to ignore it. Doing that would quietly
# drop the entire research record out of version control, and the loss would not be noticed until
# someone went looking for a round that no longer existed. The rules above are deliberately
# file-type-scoped (`data/**/*.csv` and friends) rather than a blanket `data/`; keep them that way.
#
# If a round ever does need excluding — a scratch round, a re-run — exclude that round by name.
# Do not add `data/` or `data/**`.

# `data/loop/` is the evolution loop's working tree, and is NOT tracked — with five exceptions.
#
# Read the `data/rounds/` banner above first: this is the opposite call, made for the opposite
# reason, and the two are easy to confuse. A round is a record; a generation is churn. The loop
# (`app.evolution`, see `strategy-program/EVOLUTION-LOOP.md`) mints a round directory *per
# generation* and runs until a human stops it, so pointing it at `data/rounds/` would have added
# directories without bound to a tree that is deliberately committed — which is exactly why
# `app.evolution.loop` writes its rounds under `data/loop/rounds/` instead. The genomes, the
# per-generation plans, the news cache and those rounds are all reproducible working state.
#
# What survives is the part that changed something outside the loop: which candidates were promoted,
# on what evidence, and which of them the paper desk now seats. `PROTOCOL.md`'s whole premise is that
# the record of a decision outlives the run that produced it, and a card sitting on the desk with no
# committed account of how it got there would break that. The ledger is tracked for the same reason:
# it is the multiple-comparison denominator every promotion's q-value was computed against, so
# discarding it would leave those q-values unverifiable after the fact. `amendments.jsonl` is tracked
# for the same reason as the ledger: it is the append-only record of every amendment a reviewer ever
# proposed to the loop's own config, applied or refused (`app.evolution.amend.apply_amendments`), and
# a self-modifying loop with no committed account of what it changed, and why, would not be auditable.
# `pending_seats.json` is tracked for the closest reason of all: it is the record of which promoted
# cards a *person* agreed to seat on the paper desk and which they refused. The loop files the
# requests; only a human decides them (`app.evolution.promotion.approve_pending_seat`), and a desk
# holding cards with no committed account of who approved them is exactly the state that made this
# queue necessary — 25 cards were seated automatically before it existed.
# Written as `data/loop/*` rather than `data/loop/`: git does not descend into an excluded
# *directory*, so a `!` negation under a `data/loop/` rule can never re-include anything and the
# four exceptions below would silently do nothing. Excluding the directory's *contents* leaves the
# directory itself visible, which is what makes the negations effective. Verified with
# `git check-ignore -v` rather than assumed -- the first spelling looked correct and ignored all three.
data/loop/*
!data/loop/promotions.jsonl
!data/loop/roster_overlay.json
!data/loop/ledger.jsonl
!data/loop/pending_seats.json
!data/loop/amendments.jsonl

# Agent Desk runs: one directory per run holding a `run.json` snapshot and the stage markdown. These
# are the working evidence of a model run -- reproducible only in the auditable sense `app.agents`'
# own docstring describes, and accumulating one directory per click -- not part of the programme's
# record. The `AgentCall` archive they point into lives under `data/loop/agent_calls/`, already
# covered by the `data/loop/*` rule above.
data/agentdesk/

# Auto-proposed cards are working output, not library members. A card earns a tracked home by being
# promoted into `candidates/` (`app.evolution.promotion`, gated by EVOLUTION-LOOP.md section 4); the
# generated tier is where the other few hundred candidates of a long search live, and committing them
# would bury the ~35 hand-authored cards the library exists to hold.
backend/app/paper/library/generated/

# Derived stores and caches — rebuildable from the raw exports
data/cache/
data/store/
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.parquet

